{
 "name": "antiarrhythmic drugs overview",
 "width": 1756,
 "title": "Antiarrhythmic drugs: stable patients get drugs, unstable get electricity, arrested get CPR, electricity and drugs; after treating precipitants, AV-nodal drugs (beta blocker, calcium channel blocker, digoxin, adenosine) serve supraventricular rhythms and channel blockers (class I procainamide and lidocaine, class III amiodarone and sotalol) reach ventricular ones; each drug's use and what rules it out, plus torsades de pointes caused by QT-prolonging drugs",
